## Git workflow

- Work happens on short-lived feature branches, one per issue slice (e.g.
  `0002-per-app-volume-control`).
- Merge into `main` via pull request.
- No direct commits to `main`.

## Testing

- Unit test (XCTest) all pure logic: gain-mapping math (slider % → applied gain), the
  above-100% soft-limiter curve, and per-app settings save/restore logic keyed by bundle ID.
- Do NOT unit test the live Core Audio Process Tap integration itself (tap creation,
  audio-activity signals, applying gain to a real stream) — verify this manually against each
  issue's acceptance criteria instead.
- No enforced code-coverage percentage threshold. Correctness of the pure-logic boundary is the
  goal, not a coverage number.

## Security

- Never commit secrets, API keys, or credentials of any kind.
- SoundUp is fully offline: no telemetry, no crash reporting, no network calls of any kind from
  the app itself.
- Captured/tapped audio is never logged, recorded, persisted, or transmitted anywhere — the
  audio-capture permission is used only to read levels and apply gain in real time.
- Per-app settings persisted to disk (bundle ID → volume/boost/mute) contain no sensitive data;
  plain local storage is sufficient, no encryption-at-rest requirement.

## Security severity floor & OSINT scope

- No Critical or High severity security findings may ship in a release. Medium/Low findings are
  tracked but do not block a release.
- OSINT scope for security review is limited to the SoundUp GitHub repository and its companion
  Homebrew tap repository. No other domains, apps, or infrastructure are in scope.

## Code style

- Follow standard Swift API Design Guidelines.
- Use default `swift-format` / Xcode formatting; no custom linter or bespoke style ruleset.
- Build the menu-bar UI using SwiftUI's `MenuBarExtra`, not manual `NSStatusItem` bridging.

## Deploy & runtime

Not applicable. SoundUp is a local native macOS app with no server, no backend, and no
environment variables. Its only distribution surface is a GitHub Release plus a companion
Homebrew tap, covered by Issue 0007 in `docs/issues/0001-soundup/`.
